Immediate action on climate change can prevent trillions in future economic losses by mitigating extreme weather events and ecosystem collapse, which are already costing billions annually.
Investing in renewable energy and green technology creates jobs and stimulates economic growth, offering long-term benefits that outweigh initial costs.
Delaying action increases the risk of irreversible environmental damage, which could lead to catastrophic economic and social consequences that are far more expensive to address later.
Many countries have already seen economic benefits from transitioning to sustainable practices, proving that proactive measures can be both environmentally and economically advantageous.
The cost of inaction includes not only financial losses but also health risks, displacement of communities, and loss of biodiversity, which have profound social and economic impacts.